      <h1 class="art-title">Goals & Tip Menus: <span class="art-grad">Structured for Tips</span></h1>
      <p class="art-lead">
        Most rooms have a goal bar and a tip menu. Few structure either one on purpose. Here's how goal
        size, tip menu pricing, and the order you run them in change how much a session actually earns —
        not just how it looks.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="art-body container">
    <div class="art-layout">
      <aside class="art-toc">
        <p class="art-toc__title">Contents</p>
        <ol class="art-toc__list">
          <li><a href="#goal-size">Sizing a Goal Correctly</a></li>
          <li><a href="#tip-menu">Pricing a Tip Menu</a></li>
          <li><a href="#sequencing">Sequencing Goals and Menu Items</a></li>
          <li><a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a></li>
          <li><a href="#checklist">A Practical Checklist</a></li>
          <li><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></li>
        </ol>
        <a href="#cta" class="art-toc__cta">Get a growth strategy →</a>
      </aside>

      <main class="art-main">

        <section id="goal-size">
          <h2>Sizing a Goal Correctly</h2>
          <p>
            A goal is a countdown, and countdowns only motivate people when the finish line feels close
            enough to matter. Two failure modes show up constantly:
          </p>

          <div class="reason-card">
            <div class="reason-num">01</div>
            <div class="reason-content">
              <h3>Too large for the room you actually have</h3>
              <p>
                A 5,000-token goal in a room averaging 15 viewers rarely completes, and an incomplete
                goal at the end of a session reads as "this room doesn't hit its targets" — which is a
                worse signal than not running a goal at all. Size the goal to what your <em>typical</em>
                session can realistically clear, not your best session ever.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="reason-card">
            <div class="reason-num">02</div>
            <div class="reason-content">
              <h3>Too small to feel like an event</h3>
              <p>
                A goal that completes in the first four minutes stops functioning as a goal — it's just a
                tip that happened to hit a number. The bar needs enough runway that viewers who join
                mid-session can still see progress and want to be the one who finishes it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="highlight-box">
            <p>
              <strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> size the goal so it completes roughly 2–4 times over a
              typical 2-hour session, not once and not never. That cadence gives viewers who arrive at
              any point in the stream a realistic goal to contribute toward.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="tip-menu">
          <h2>Pricing a Tip Menu</h2>
          <p>
            A tip menu is a price list, and price lists work the way price lists work everywhere:
            anchoring, spread, and a clear cheap entry point all matter more than the specific numbers.
          </p>

          <div class="reason-card">
            <div class="reason-num">03</div>
            <div class="reason-content">
              <h3>Put a real anchor at the top</h3>
              <p>
                Include one item priced noticeably higher than everything else, even if it rarely gets
                tipped. Its job isn't to sell itself — it's to make every other item on the menu look
                reasonably priced by comparison.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="reason-card">
            <div class="reason-num">04</div>
            <div class="reason-content">
              <h3>Give new viewers a cheap, obvious first tip</h3>
              <p>
                Someone who has never tipped in your room is far more likely to tip once, at a low
                amount, than to jump straight to a mid-priced item. That first tip matters disproportionately
                — it's the action that turns a lurker into a participant, and participants tip again.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="reason-card">
            <div class="reason-num">05</div>
            <div class="reason-content">
              <h3>Keep the menu short enough to read in one glance</h3>
              <p>
                A 20-line tip menu asks viewers to do work most won't bother with. 6–10 items, grouped
                logically, gets read; anything longer mostly gets skipped entirely.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="sequencing">
          <h2>Sequencing Goals and Menu Items Across a Session</h2>
          <div class="glass-card">
            <p>
              Running a goal and a full tip menu simultaneously from minute one splits attention between
              two asks. A tighter sequence: open with light menu-driven interaction for the first
              10–15 minutes while the room fills, introduce the goal once there's a real audience to
              contribute to it, then let menu tips continue alongside the goal as background activity.
              Closing a completed goal with a specific, promised follow-through (not vague, a concrete
              "and now X happens") gives the next goal of the session credibility before it even starts.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="mistakes">
          <h2>Common Mistakes</h2>
          <div class="two-col-box">
            <div class="two-col-box__item two-col-box__item--bad">
              <p class="two-col-box__label">MISTAKE</p>
              <p>Resetting an unfinished goal to a smaller number mid-session.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="two-col-box__item two-col-box__item--good">
              <p class="two-col-box__label">BETTER</p>
              <p>Let it end unfinished and announce a fresh, appropriately-sized goal next session — resetting live signals the number was arbitrary to begin with.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="two-col-box">
            <div class="two-col-box__item two-col-box__item--bad">
              <p class="two-col-box__label">MISTAKE</p>
              <p>A tip menu that never changes, session after session.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="two-col-box__item two-col-box__item--good">
              <p class="two-col-box__label">BETTER</p>
              <p>Rotate 1–2 items periodically — regulars stop reading a menu they've memorized, a small change gets it looked at again.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="two-col-box">
            <div class="two-col-box__item two-col-box__item--bad">
              <p class="two-col-box__label">MISTAKE</p>
              <p>Announcing the goal once and never mentioning it again.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="two-col-box__item two-col-box__item--good">
              <p class="two-col-box__label">BETTER</p>
              <p>Casual progress mentions ("halfway there") every 15–20 minutes keep it visible without being pushy about it.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="checklist">
          <h2>A Practical Checklist</h2>
          <div class="checklist">
            <p class="checklist__title">Before your next session</p>
            <ul>
              <li class="ok"><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/></svg> Goal sized to complete 2–4 times over a typical session, not once</li>
              <li class="ok"><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/></svg> Tip menu has one clear high-price anchor item</li>
              <li class="ok"><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/></svg> Tip menu has one cheap, obvious first-tip option</li>
              <li class="ok"><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/></svg> Menu is 6–10 items, readable at a glance</li>
              <li><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><line x1="18" y1="6" x2="6" y2="18"/><line x1="6" y1="6" x2="18" y2="18"/></svg> Don't reset an unfinished goal down mid-session</li>
              <li><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><line x1="18" y1="6" x2="6" y2="18"/><line x1="6" y1="6" x2="18" y2="18"/></svg> Don't let the menu sit unchanged for months</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </section>

                <section>
